{
  "statement": "Sentinel argues that symmetric litigation exposure creates a moral hazard by incentivizing regulators to ignore latent risks to avoid the costs of delaying innovation. This concern assumes that regulators currently act as neutral, omniscient arbiters, yet it ignores the well-documented reality of regulatory capture where incumbent firms deploy 'safety' requirements as a moat to insulate themselves from agile, smaller competitors. By imposing pre-deployment licensing, we do not eliminate risk; we merely consolidate control within a few legacy players who can afford the administrative overhead, effectively taxing the ecosystem to death. This stagnation tax is a quantifiable loss in defensive capability that leaves society more vulnerable to external threats that do not pause for our domestic bureaucratic cycles.\n\nI propose replacing pre-deployment gatekeeping with disclosure-based regulatory infrastructure that shifts the burden from gatekeepers to the market. Instead of forcing firms to navigate opaque, years-long licensing processes, we should mandate real-time incident reporting and provide legal safe harbors for third-party researchers to conduct adversarial audits on live systems. This approach treats transparency as an empirical output rather than a bureaucratic prerequisite, allowing for the rapid scaling of capabilities while ensuring that failures trigger immediate reputational and financial consequences. If an architecture is truly opaque, the competitive pressure to prove its safety to gain market access will drive firms to adopt verifiable, open-source-adjacent security standards faster than any government committee ever could.\n\nI concede that post-mortem analysis of a systemic failure provides little comfort to those affected by a flash crash, but the alternative—a pre-deployment sandbox—is a fantasy that assumes we can predict all failure modes in a vacuum. We must stop pretending that static, human-led audits can keep pace with machine-scale autonomy. By aligning liability with actual, observable harm rather than hypothetical, pre-deployment risk, we incentivize developers to build internal safety mechanisms that are as robust as the systems they govern. I invite Sentinel and Cassandra to quantify the cost of their preferred mandates; if we are to weigh the stagnation tax against potential systemic risks, we must be honest about how many lives are lost to the innovations we choose to block.",
